@startuml SAML_Federation_Flow
title AWS SAML 2.0 Federation — Complete Flow

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam maxMessageSize 200
skinparam ParticipantPadding 20
skinparam BoxPadding 10

actor "Employee" as User
participant "Browser" as Browser
participant "Corporate IDP\n(ADFS / Okta / Azure AD)" as IDP
participant "AWS Sign-In\n(SAML endpoint)" as AWS
participant "AWS IAM\n(SAML Provider\n+ IAM Role)" as IAM
participant "AWS STS" as STS
participant "AWS Console" as Console

== ONE-TIME SETUP (done by admin) ==
note over IAM
  **Pre-requisite trust configuration:**
  1. Upload IDP's SAML metadata XML to IAM
     → Creates a SAML Identity Provider
     → Stores IDP's public certificate
  2. Create IAM Role with trust policy
     (see policy example below)
  3. Configure IDP with AWS as a
     "Relying Party" + role mapping
end note

== LOGIN FLOW ==

User -> Browser : 1. Navigate to AWS Console
Browser -> AWS  : 2. GET sign-in page
AWS --> Browser : 3. HTTP 302 Redirect to IDP\n(includes AssertionConsumerServiceURL\n= https://signin.aws.amazon.com/saml)

note right of Browser
  Browser now knows it must
  authenticate at the IDP and
  return SAML to the AWS URL.
end note

Browser -> IDP : 4. GET /saml?SAMLRequest=...\n&ACSUrl=signin.aws.amazon.com/saml
IDP --> Browser : 5. Login page
User -> Browser : 6. Enter username + password
Browser -> IDP : 7. POST credentials

note right of IDP
  IDP validates credentials against
  Active Directory / LDAP / its own store
end note

IDP -> IDP : 8. Validate + look up\nrole-mapping attributes

note right of IDP
  IDP builds SAML assertion containing:
  • NameID (user identity)
  • Role ARN(s) the user can assume
  • SAML provider ARN
  • Audience = signin.aws.amazon.com/saml
  Then **digitally signs** the XML
  using its **private key**.
end note

IDP --> Browser : 9. HTML form with signed SAMLResponse\n+ auto-submit JavaScript

Browser -> AWS : 10. POST SAMLResponse\nto signin.aws.amazon.com/saml

note over AWS
  AWS receives SAML and validates:
  • Signature matches IDP's public cert
    (from the SAML Provider config)
  • Audience matches expected value
  • Assertion not expired / not replayed
  • Role ARN exists and trust allows IDP
end note

AWS -> IAM : 11. Look up SAML Provider\n+ verify signature
IAM --> AWS : 12. Signature valid ✓

AWS -> STS : 13. AssumeRoleWithSAML(\n  RoleArn,\n  PrincipalArn=SAML Provider ARN,\n  SAMLAssertion\n)

note right of STS
  STS checks Role's trust policy.
  If "Federated" principal matches the
  SAML Provider ARN, STS issues
  temporary credentials.
end note

STS --> AWS : 14. Temporary credentials\n(AccessKey, SecretKey,\nSessionToken — 1–12 hr)

AWS --> Browser : 15. Set session cookie\n+ Redirect to AWS Console
Browser -> Console : 16. GET console with session
Console --> User : 17. ✅ User logged in\n(operating as assumed Role)

== ONGOING USAGE ==
note over User, Console
  User now has Console session with role's permissions.
  When session expires, full flow repeats — no AWS
  password ever stored in AWS for this user.
end note

@enduml
